@node starting sbcl
@section Starting SBCL

@menu
* Running without Installation: running without installation.
* Running from Emacs: running from emacs.
* Shebang Scripts: shebang scripts.
* Parts of SBCL: parts of sbcl.
* Finding Parts of SBCL: finding parts of sbcl.
@end menu

If SBCL is installed properly and it is in your @code{path}, you can simply
type @code{sbcl} at the command line. You should end up in the toplevel
@emph{REPL} (read-eval-print loop), where you can interact with SBCL by
typing expressions.

@example
$ sbcl
This is SBCL 0.8.13.60, an implementation of ANSI Common Lisp.
More information about SBCL is available at <http://www.sbcl.org/>.

SBCL is free software, provided as is, with absolutely no warranty.
It is mostly in the public domain; some portions are provided under
BSD-style licenses.  See the CREDITS and COPYING files in the
distribution for more information.
* (+ 2 2)
4
* (exit)
$
@end example

See also @ref{command line options} and @ref{stopping sbcl}.

@node running without installation
@subsection Running without Installation

You can quickly run SBCL without installing it by invoking the
@code{run-sbcl.sh} script. This works both with an unpacked
@ref{binary distribution} and after @ref{building sbcl} yourself:

@example
$ sh run-sbcl.sh
@end example

@node running from emacs
@subsection Running from Emacs

To run SBCL as an @code{inferior-lisp} from Emacs, add something like this
to your @code{.emacs}:

@example
;;; The SBCL binary and command-line arguments
(setq inferior-lisp-program "/usr/local/bin/sbcl --noinform")
@end example

For more information on using SBCL with Emacs, see
@ref{editor integration}.

@node shebang scripts
@subsection Shebang Scripts

Standard Unix tools that are interpreters follow a common command-line
protocol that is necessary to work with "shebang scripts". SBCL
supports this via the @code{--script} command-line option (see
@ref{command line options}).

Example file (@code{hello.lisp}):

@example
#!/usr/local/bin/sbcl --script
(write-line "Hello, World!")
@end example

Usage from the command line:

@example
$ ./hello.lisp
Hello, World!
@end example

Note that SBCL skips the shebang line when it reads the file:

@example
$ sbcl --script hello.lisp
Hello, World!
@end example

@node parts of sbcl
@subsection Parts of SBCL

If you only ever use SBCL packaged for your operating system,
run it from an installation created with @code{install.sh}, run it with @code{run-sbcl.sh}, there is no need to worry about the
files necessary at run-time. If you, for example, want to install
SBCL manually, package it in a way that deviates from the file
hierarchy created by @code{install.sh}, or run SBCL with your own core
file saved by @code{sb-ext:save-lisp-and-die}, then read on.

The following files play a part in running SBCL.

@itemize
@item @emph{The @code{sbcl} binary}

This is a standard executable built by compiling and linking an
ordinary C program. It provides the runtime environment for the
running Lisp image, but it doesn't know much about high-level
Lisp stuff (like symbols and printing and objects), so it's
pretty useless by itself.

The binary is @code{src/runtime/sbcl} in @ref{binary distribution}s and
@ref{source distribution}s; and it is installed
to @code{/usr/local/bin/sbcl} by default.

@item @emph{The @code{sbcl.core} file}

This is an image dump written in a
special SBCL format that only SBCL understands, and it contains
all the high-level Lisp stuff.

The core file is @code{obj/sbcl-home/sbcl.core} in distributions;
installed to @code{/usr/local/lib/sbcl/sbcl.core} by default. In
addition, there are a number of @ref{contributed modules} that extend
the basic SBCL functionality.

@item @emph{The @code{contrib/} directory}

This holds the compiled fasl files for the @ref{contributed modules},
to be loaded on demand.

The contrib fasl files are in @code{obj/sbcl-home/contrib/} in
distributions; installed to @code{/usr/local/lib/sbcl/contrib/} by
default.
@end itemize

@quotation
@emph{Note}: Only the binary and the core file are absolutely necessary
to start SBCL; the contrib fasls are optional.
@end quotation

@node finding parts of sbcl
@subsection Finding Parts of SBCL

When you invoke the SBCL binary, it needs to be able to find the other
@ref{parts of sbcl}, namely the @code{sbcl.core} file and the
@ref{contributed modules} in the @code{contrib/} directory.

@itemize
@item Finding @code{sbcl.core}

If the @code{--core} runtime option is specified,
then its value is used. Example:

@example
$ sbcl --core /foo/bar/sbcl.core
@end example

Else, the runtime looks for @code{sbcl.core} in the following
directories until the first match.

@itemize
@item The directory denoted by the @code{SBCL_HOME} environment
      variable if it is set

@item The current directory

@item The directory of the @code{sbcl} binary

@item The @code{../lib/sbcl/} directory relative to the @code{sbcl} binary
@end itemize

If no core file was found by the above rules, then the runtime
exits with an error message.

@item Finding @code{contrib/}

Once the runtime loads the core file, the Lisp looks for
@code{contrib/} in the following directories until the first match.

@itemize
@item The directory denoted by the @code{SBCL_HOME} environment
      variable if it is set

@item @emph{The directory of @code{sbcl.core}}

@item @emph{The directory @code{../lib/sbcl} relative to @code{sbcl.core}}

@item The current directory

@item The directory of the @code{sbcl} binary

@item The directory @code{../lib/sbcl} relative to the @code{sbcl} binary
@end itemize

The list of directories is the same as for finding the core file
except here we also try directories relative to @code{sbcl.core}.

If the @code{contrib/} directory is not found, then no error is
signalled immediately, but loading modules will fail.
@end itemize

@node stopping sbcl
@section Stopping SBCL

@menu
* Exit: exit.
* End of File: end of file.
* Exit on Errors: exit on errors.
@end menu

@node exit
@subsection Exit

SBCL can be stopped at any time by calling @code{sb-ext:exit},
optionally returning a specified numeric value to the calling
process. See @ref{threading} for information about terminating individual
threads.

@node end of file
@subsection End of File

By default, SBCL also exits upon reaching the end of input. This can
be caused either by the user pressing @code{Control-D} on an attached
terminal, or by the input stream ending when SBCL is used as part of
a shell pipeline.

@node exit on errors
@subsection Exit on Errors

SBCL can also be configured to exit if an unhandled error occurs,
which is mainly useful for acting as part of a shell pipeline; doing
so under most other circumstances would mean giving up large parts
of the flexibility and robustness of Common Lisp. See
@ref{debugger entry} and the command-line option @code{--disable-debugger} in
@ref{toplevel options}.

@node command line options
@section Command-line Options

@menu
* Runtime Options: runtime options.
* Toplevel Options: toplevel options.
@end menu

Command-line options can be considered an advanced topic; for ordinary
interactive use, no command-line arguments should be necessary.

In order to understand the command-line argument syntax for SBCL, it
is helpful to understand that the SBCL system is implemented as two
components, a low-level runtime environment written in C and a
higher-level system written in Common Lisp. Arguments are processed
during the respective initializations of the runtime and Lisp
system, with any remainder available to user code via
@code{sb-ext:*posix-argv*}.

The full, unambiguous syntax for invoking SBCL at the command line
is:

@example
sbcl <runtime-option>* --end-runtime-options \
     <toplevel-option>* --end-toplevel-options \
     <user-option>*
@end example

The @code{--end-runtime-options} and @code{--end-toplevel-options} can be
omitted, provided that no ambiguities are possible with the option
values used. Omitting these elements is probably a bad idea for any
batch file where any of the options are under user control because
it makes it impossible for SBCL to detect erroneous command-line
arguments, which will then be passed on to the user program even if
they were intended for the runtime system or the Lisp system.

@node runtime options
@subsection Runtime Options

@cindex disabling LDB
@cindex LDB, disabling
@itemize
@item @code{--core <corefilename>}

Run the specified Lisp core file instead of the default. Note
that if the Lisp core file is a user-created core file, it may
run a nonstandard toplevel that does not recognize the standard
toplevel options.

@item @code{--dynamic-space-size <size>}

Size of the dynamic space reserved on startup. The default value
is platform-dependent. The units supported are kB (or KiB),
MB (or MiB), GB (or GiB), and TB (or TiB). The names are
compared case-insensitively, and the default unit is megabytes.
Thus, @code{8}, @code{8mb}, and @code{8mib} all mean 8 * 2^20 bytes.

@item @code{--control-stack-size <size>}

Size of control stack reserved for each thread. The @code{<size>}
argument has the same format as in @code{--dynamic-space-size}. The
default is 2MB.

@item @code{--tls-limit <positive integer>}

Maximum number of thread-local @ref{special variables} in threaded
builds. Default value is 4096.

@item @code{--noinform}

Suppress the printing of any banner or other informational
message at startup. This makes it easier to write Lisp programs
which work cleanly in Unix pipelines. See also the @code{--noprint}
and @code{--disable-debugger} options.

@item @code{--disable-ldb}

Disable the @ref{ldb}, the low-level debugger. Only effective if SBCL
is compiled with LDB. When LDB is not available or is
disabled, SBCL exits with a non-zero exit code whenever it would
enter LDB. 

@item @code{--lose-on-corruption}

There are some dangerous low-level errors (for instance, control
stack exhausted, memory fault) that (or whose handlers) can
corrupt the image. By default, SBCL prints a warning, then tries
to continue and handle the error in Lisp. However, this will not
always work, and SBCL may subsequently malfunction or even hang.
With this option, upon encountering such an error, SBCL will
exit instead of signalling a Lisp error.

@item @code{--script <filename>}

As a @emph{runtime} option, this is equivalent to @code{--noinform}
@code{--disable-ldb} @code{--lose-on-corruption}
@code{--end-runtime-options} @code{--script} @code{<filename>}. See
the description of @code{--script} as a @emph{toplevel} option below.
If there are no other command-line arguments following
@code{--script}, the filename argument can be omitted.

@item @code{--merge-core-pages}

When platform support is present, provide hints to the operating
system that identical pages may be shared between processes
until they are written to. This can be useful to reduce the
memory usage on systems with multiple SBCL processes started
from similar but differently-named core files, or from
compressed cores. Without platform support, do nothing. By
default only compressed cores trigger hinting.

@item @code{--no-merge-core-pages}

Ensures that no sharing hint is provided to the operating
system.

@item @code{--help}

Print some basic information about SBCL, then exit.

@item @code{--version}

Print SBCL's version information, then exit.

@item @code{--end-runtime-options}

End processing of runtime options and pass the rest of the
command-line arguments to the toplevel function.
@end itemize

In the future, runtime options may be added to control behaviour
such as lazy allocation of memory.

@node toplevel options
@subsection Toplevel Options

The following options are supported by the @ref{default toplevel}. If the
toplevel function was overriden with @code{sb-ext:save-lisp-and-die}, the
set of available toplevel options is likely very different.

@itemize
@item @code{--sysinit <filename>}

Load @code{<filename>} instead of the default system initialization
file (see @ref{initialization files}).

@item @code{--no-sysinit}

Don't load a system-wide initialization file. If this option is
given, the @code{--sysinit} option is ignored.

@item @code{--userinit <filename>}

Load @code{<filename>} instead of the default user initialization
file (see @ref{initialization files}.)

@item @code{--no-userinit}

Don't load a user initialization file. If this option is given,
the @code{--userinit} option is ignored.

@item @code{--eval <command>}

After executing any initialization file, but before starting the
read-eval-print loop on standard input, read and evaluate
@code{<command>}. More than one @code{--eval} option can be used, and all
will be read and executed, in the order they appear on the
command line.

@item @code{--load <filename>}

This is equivalent to @code{--eval '(load "<filename>")'}. The
special syntax is intended to reduce quoting headaches when
invoking SBCL from shell scripts.

@item @code{--noprint}

When ordinarily the toplevel "read-eval-print loop" would be
executed, execute a "read-eval loop" instead, i.e. don't print
a prompt and don't echo results. Combined with the @code{--noinform}
runtime option, this makes it easier to write Lisp scripts that
work cleanly in Unix pipelines.

@item @code{--disable-debugger}

By default, when SBCL encounters an error, it enters the builtin
debugger, allowing interactive diagnosis and possible
intercession. This option disables the debugger, causing errors
to print a backtrace and exit with status 1 instead. When given,
this option takes effect before loading initialization files or
processing @code{--eval} and @code{--load} options. See
@code{sb-ext:disable-debugger} and @ref{debugger entry}.

@item @code{--script <filename>}

Implies @code{--no-userinit} @code{--no-sysinit} @code{--disable-debugger}
@code{--end-toplevel-options}.

Causes the system to load the specified file instead of entering
the read-eval-print-loop, and exit afterwards. If the file
begins with a shebang line, it is ignored.

If there are no other command-line arguments following, the
filename can be omitted: this causes the script to be loaded
from standard input instead. In this case, shebang lines are
currently @emph{not} ignored.

In either case, if there is an unhandled error (such as an end
of file condition or a broken pipe) on standard input, standard
output, or standard error, the script silently exits with code
0. This allows piping output from SBCL to another command
without unexpected errors.

Additionally, the option sets @code{*compile-verbose*} and
@code{*load-verbose*} to @code{nil} while loading the file to avoid
potentially verbose diagnostic messages printed on the standard
output.

@item @code{--end-toplevel-options}

End processing of toplevel options, leaving the rest of the
command-line arguments in @code{sb-ext:*posix-argv*}.
@end itemize

@node initialization files
@section Initialization Files

The @ref{default toplevel} supports customization via the system and user
initialization files. These files with @code{read} and @code{eval}, not @code{load};
hence initialization files can be used to set startup @code{*package*} and
@code{*readtable*}, and for proclaiming a global optimization policy.

@cindex Read-Eval-Print Loop
@cindex REPL
@itemize
@item @strong{System Initialization File:} Defaults to @code{sbclrc} in the
  directory where @code{contrib/} is found (see @ref{finding parts of sbcl}),
  or if that doesn't exist to @code{/etc/sbclrc}. Can be overridden with
  the command-line option @code{--sysinit} or @code{--no-sysinit} (see
  @ref{toplevel options}). Set @code{sb-ext:*sysinit-pathname-function*} to
  override the above defaulting behavior.

The system initialization file is intended for system
administrators and software packagers to configure locations of
installed third party modules, etc.

@item @strong{User Initialization File:} Defaults to @code{$HOME/.sbclrc}. Can be
  overridden with the command-line option @code{--userinit} or
  @code{--no-userinit} (see @ref{toplevel options}). Set
  @code{sb-ext:*userinit-pathname-function*} to override the above
  defaulting behavior.

The user initialization file is intended for personal
customizations, such as loading certain modules at startup,
defining convenience functions to use in the REPL, handling
automatic recompilation of fasls (see @ref{fasl format}), etc.
@end itemize

Neither initialization file is required.

@anchor{Variable sb-ext *sysinit-pathname-function*}
@vvindex @sortas{sysinit-pathname-function* sb-ext} *sysinit-pathname-function* [sb-ext]
@deffn{Variable} sb-ext:*sysinit-pathname-function*
Controls the sysinit file defaulting of the
@ref{default toplevel}. This is @code{nil} or a designator for a
function of zero arguments.

@itemize
@item If @code{nil}, the default behavior described in
  @ref{initialization files} is in effect.

@item If a function, it is called by the default toplevel to obtain a
  pathname designator for the default sysinit file or @code{nil}. If the
  function returns @code{nil}, no sysinit file is used unless one has been
  specified on the command line.
@end itemize
@end deffn
@anchor{Variable sb-ext *userinit-pathname-function*}
@vvindex @sortas{userinit-pathname-function* sb-ext} *userinit-pathname-function* [sb-ext]
@deffn{Variable} sb-ext:*userinit-pathname-function*
Controls the userinit file defaulting of the
@ref{default toplevel}. This is @code{nil} or a designator for a
function of zero arguments.

@itemize
@item If @code{nil}, the default behavior described in
  @ref{initialization files} is in effect.

@item If a function, it is called by the default toplevel to obtain a
  pathname designator for the default userinit file or @code{nil}. If the
  function returns @code{nil}, no sysinit file is used unless one has been
  specified on the command line.
@end itemize
@end deffn
@node initialization and exit hooks
@section Initialization and Exit Hooks

SBCL provides hooks into the system initialization and exit.

@anchor{Variable sb-ext *init-hooks*}
@vvindex @sortas{init-hooks* sb-ext} *init-hooks* [sb-ext]
@deffn{Variable} sb-ext:*init-hooks*
A list of function designators called in an unspecified
order when a saved core image starts up, after the system itself has
been initialized, but before non-user threads such as the finalizer
thread have been started.

Unused by SBCL itself; reserved for the user, contribs and applications.
@end deffn
@anchor{Variable sb-ext *exit-hooks*}
@vvindex @sortas{exit-hooks* sb-ext} *exit-hooks* [sb-ext]
@deffn{Variable} sb-ext:*exit-hooks*
A list of function designators called in an unspecified
order when SBCL process exits.

Unused by SBCL itself; reserved for the user, contribs and applications.

Using (@code{sb-ext:exit} @code{:abort} @code{t}), or calling @code{exit(3)} directly circumvents
these hooks.
@end deffn
